From 77c640aed20b25114786739ff0a59d5c373a93e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shaun Adams <s.adams@f5.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Jun 2026 20:20:15 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] transports+multitenant: tenant resolver middleware +
 ConfigStoreVKResolver
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

First load-bearing wiring of the tenant_id concept into the request
path. Three pieces, all behind interfaces so the per-tenant Bifrost
dispatcher (next patch) plugs in without further surgery.

1) multitenant.BifrostContextKeyTenantID — the context key under
   which the resolved tenant id is stored. Defined as a
   schemas.BifrostContextKey constant so any consumer can read it
   without importing multitenant beyond the constant.

2) multitenant.ConfigStoreVKResolver — implements VKResolver by
   looking up the VK in framework/configstore's
   governance_virtual_keys table via GetVirtualKeyByValue. Wraps
   nicely in CachedVKResolver for the production hot path:
       cached := NewCachedVKResolver(NewConfigStoreVKResolver(store), 60*time.Second)

   Semantics:
     - empty VK / not found / IsActive=false → ErrUnknownVK
       (collapsed so callers can't time-distinguish "wrong" vs
       "disabled")
     - empty TenantID on the row → real error (signals a row that
       escaped the backfill — should be noticed, not silently
       routed to "")
     - DB error → wrapped and returned (caller decides)

3) handlers.TenantResolverMiddleware(resolver) — fasthttp middleware
   that extracts the VK from the request (same header shapes as
   lib/ctx.go: x-bf-vk, Authorization Bearer sk-bf-*, x-api-key,
   x-goog-api-key) and writes the resolved tenant id onto the
   RequestCtx UserValue under BifrostContextKeyTenantID. Downstream
   BifrostContext construction in lib/ctx.go will lift it onto the
   inference context where the per-tenant dispatcher reads it.

   Fail-open by design at this layer:
     - missing VK / unknown VK / DB outage → no tenant id set,
       request still flows. The governance plugin owns the 401 path
       (single source of truth for VK auth/authz).
     - skip paths: /health and /api/platform/* (no VK lookup
       needed on platform-admin or health probes)
     - nil resolver → noop (the deployment hasn't wired one yet)

Tests:
  - 7 in multitenant/configstore_resolver_test.go covering happy path,
    unknown / empty / inactive VKs, missing TenantID, DB errors,
    nil-store panic
  - 8 in transports/bifrost-http/handlers/tenant_resolver_test.go
    covering each header shape, missing VK pass-through, unknown VK
    pass-through, resolver-error pass-through, skip paths, and nil
    resolver

NOT in this patch (deliberately): wiring the middleware into the
server.go middleware chain. That patch also lands the per-tenant
Bifrost runtime dispatch in the inference path, so both halves of the
end-to-end story ship together. Until then this code is dead-but-
testable scaffolding.

Ref: https://jira.f5net.com/browse/XC-25496
